From 02475c81964c59acc7c55f70d5c75198c62b6442 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <virajslko@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:34:17 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] scripting: run property scripts the way Wallpaper
 Engine does
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A property script (SceneScript attached to an object's property) never
ran. queueScript kept what JS_Eval returns for a module, which in
quickjs-ng is the evaluation's Promise, so `update`, `init` and every
event handler were looked up on the Promise and never found; the
"result" (undefined) was then written into the property as null every
frame: scripted origins went to (0,0), scripted alpha/visible/scale to 0.
Underneath, the runtime the scripts would have met was broken too.

Property scripts now:

- Compile with JS_EVAL_FLAG_COMPILE_ONLY, evaluate with JS_EvalFunction
  (a rejected evaluation is a load error) and keep the module namespace
  (JS_GetModuleNamespace), where update/init/the events are found.
- Evaluate on the first tick after they were queued, when the whole scene
  exists (objects queue them from their constructors), in queue order;
  then init(value) once each, then applyUserProperties(<every user
  property>) once (WE calls it on load; later changes don't reach it yet),
  then update(value) every tick.
- Write back only a value: undefined/null (no `return`) leave the property
  alone, as WE documents. A returned Vec3/Vec4 keeps its z and w (they
  were dropped to 0). A returned object without numeric x/y is ignored
  (it used to throw a C++ exception into the frame).
- See their own layer: `thisLayer` and `thisObject` are module-scope
  bindings of the object the script is attached to (they were one global,
  the last object that queued a script; thisObject didn't exist), so event
  handlers and timer callbacks keep them.
- Bind scale/angles/visible to the values the object renders with: the
  ScriptableObject base registered the object's group* copies first and
  the typed registration was dropped. A text object's angles stay the
  group value (it has none of its own), and its text script runs only as
  the text layer script (it ran a second time as a property script).
- Log what they throw: once per error up to five per script, with the
  module key, the function and the stack (they were silent).

The JS they run against is Wallpaper Engine's own:

- Before anything else the engine evaluates WE's
  scripts/jsclasses/baseclasses.js (Vec2/Vec3/Vec4/Mat3/Mat4 with all
  their methods, createScriptProperties, _Internal) as WE does, and
  `import … from 'WEMath' | 'WEColor' | 'WEVector'` loads
  scripts/jsmodules/<name>.js. Both are read at run time from the user's
  WE assets (the assets dir LWE already mounts), never shipped. Without
  them, bare Vec2/Vec3/Vec4 constructors stand in (written from the
  documented API) and the modules don't resolve.
- So the native vector adapters (only argv[0] used, so new Vec3(1,2,3)
  was (1,1,1); new Vec3() threw; every method unreachable behind the
  exotic getter; no reflect/Mat*) and the native WEMath/WEColor modules
  (threw on any argument count but 3) are gone; the C++ side only
  marshals: a DynamicValue vector becomes `new VecN(...)` of WE's class.
- createScriptProperties() is WE's builder (every key gets its default; a
  combo its first option) with the scene's stored values laid over it
  live, a string for a vector default becoming that vector (WE's
  _Internal.updateScriptProperties). The stored values are those of the
  module being evaluated (they were the previous module's, or none).
- thisLayer / getLayer() objects read their registered properties and
  `name`, and writing a registered property sets it (the exotic get/set
  were never installed: every read was undefined). Other names are
  accepted and dropped.
- engine.frametime/runtime/timeOfDay, setTimeout, setInterval and
  createScriptProperties are JS_CFUNC_generic_magic functions (they were
  registered as plain generic ones and read a garbage magic), and the
  engine object registers itself (it never did, so every engine call
  failed without an error). Timer callbacks are owned (JS_DupValue; they
  were freed by whoever passed them), their results freed and exceptions
  logged, a callback may clear or add timers, and the stop function
  setTimeout/setInterval return works without arguments (it read its id
  from argv[0]).
- A text layer's `thisScene` and `engine` are the real objects behind its
  own clock fields (getLayer, setInterval, … threw "not a function").
- thisScene.getLayer() of a name it doesn't find is undefined (it was an
  exception without an error).

Not done (later batches): cursor and most media events, live
applyUserProperties and engine.userProperties, registerAudioBuffers,
layer methods (getTextureAnimation, getAnimation, size, …), sound layers,
createLayer/sortLayer, scripts on effect/material/particle values and on
group objects, persistent localStorage.

Origin: ours
Why: no property script ran in lp-player: scripted layers static, at (0,0), invisible or black in 40 of the owner's scenes (docs/player/spikes/we-lwe-gap-audit.md B1: S1-S7, S9, S33-S35, S38)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vg3T4gecqrPRHLwoGGiWVa
